B3MIZuHBk2z.content:
My first International Surf Rescue Challenge is complete. I am overfilling with gratitude for the opportunity that was provided to for me to represent my country in #surflifesavingsport by #stokedtogoout , #wbor , @sala_usla , @uslifesaving and all of you beautiful people who supported me with your kind wishes as well as financially.
I am in awe to witness and to compete against such excellence. Not only are these athletes and coaches so talented with an amazing work ethic but they are true students and masters of their events. 
On top of that, the South Africans were so welcoming. I can't say that I've been to another country where people were so hospitable and friendly to me regardless of race. 
South Africa is a melting pot much like America. They have at least 11 official languages. There are blacks, whites, Indians, Middle-Easterners, as well as everything in-between. They have their race issues as well. But the best people navigate toxic tribalism by expressing kindness, being examples of loving human beings, and not judging people by the color of their skin. 
This visit has made me proud to be an American though. The opportunity that we have in this country to be successful I believe is unparalleled. As an American, if you have a strong work ethic and a dream you can make it happen.  Nothing is perfect though and there is always room for improvement for civil rights in our country. 
Through this traveling experience I will not take for granted my freedom to be my best self. I also think that it will be even easier for me to see the best in people no matter their class or race. 
A lesson that I can pass along is to work hard, chase your dreams, act with your heart and not your ego, lift others up, and give sincere compliments as much as possible because we only have so much time together on this beautiful planet we call Earth. 
